<BaseLayout title="Teaching | Trevor Smith, Ph.D." description="Teaching philosophy, robotics education, undergraduate mentorship, and tools for making academic research practice explicit.">
  <section class="page-hero teaching-hero shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">Teaching Philosophy</p><blockquote>Hands-on experience, freedom to explore, and the freedom to make mistakes are critical to learning and innovation.</blockquote><p>This bottom-up philosophy mirrors my research. I use hands-on systems, transparent feedback, and open-ended projects to help students connect mathematics to machines. When a model moves a motor, coordinates a swarm, or fails in an instructive way, the “why” becomes tangible.</p></div><img src={vexConstruction.src} alt="Students constructing a VEX competition robot in the lab"></section>
  <section class="teaching-feature"><div class="feature-image"><img src="/images/teaching/smartbots.jpg" alt="Mobile robot platforms developed for teaching robotics"></div><div class="feature-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Course development · MAE 412: Mobile Robotics</p><h2>SMARTbots</h2><p class="lead">20 custom educational robots built to make autonomy physical and approachable.</p><p>I aided in designing, fabricating, and programming a classroom fleet for WVU’s Mobile Robotics course. The platforms give students direct experience with navigation, sensor fusion, kinematics, manipulation, and the persistence required to debug real hardware.</p><ul class="tags"><li>Navigation</li><li>Sensor fusion</li><li>Kinematics</li><li>Manipulation</li></ul></div></section>
  <section class="mentor shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">Experiential mentorship</p><h2>From team leader to mentor.</h2><p>Across the University Rover Challenge, WVU Robotics Club, undergraduate research, and an NSF Research Experience for Undergraduates program, I have mentored students through design, manufacturing, software integration, experimental practice, and scientific communication.</p><div class="metric-row"><div><strong>40+</strong><span>students on an interdisciplinary rover team</span></div><div><strong>9</strong><span>undergraduate researchers across disciplines</span></div><div><strong>1st</strong><span>international URC finish in 2023</span></div></div><p>Mentees have earned publication co-authorship, moved into team leadership, entered graduate programs, and taken positions at SpaceX, Blue Origin, and MITRE.</p></div><img src="/images/teaching/urc-team.jpg" alt="University Rover Challenge students working together in the lab"></section>
  <section class="minion-section"><div class="shell minion-grid"><div><img src="/images/teaching/minion.png" alt="Minion research assistant interface"></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Academic process design</p><h2>Minion: Your Research Assistant</h2><p class="lead">A unified workspace for navigating research from the first question to final dissemination.</p><p>For new researchers, Minion reveals the “unknown unknowns” of academic work; for experienced researchers, it reduces fragmentation and amplifies productivity.</p><p>Minion connects project planning, literature discovery and reference management, Jupyter notebooks, LaTeX writing, and dissemination guidance. It keeps nonlinear work focused by making each project’s core question, goals, intellectual merit, and broader impacts explicit.</p><p class="availability">Developing project · Additional demonstrations and documentation forthcoming</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
